Notebook[
 {Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{{Cell["HEPCAT SYMBOL", "PacletNameCell"],
       Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["HEPCAT", BaseStyle -> "Link",
           ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/guide/HEPCAT"], "  \[Bullet]  ",
          ButtonBox["Related Guide", BaseStyle -> "Link",
           ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/guide/AmplitudeConstruction"]}],
        "AnchorBar"]}}, GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {Left, Right}},
     GridBoxItemSize -> {"Columns" -> {Scaled[0.42], Scaled[0.58]}}]],
   "AnchorBarGrid"], Cell["CompleteHighEnergy", "ObjectName"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Use high-energy and other full-amplitude conditions to fix \
freedom left after physical factorization."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"CompleteHighEnergy", "[", "input",
         ",", " ", "channels", ",", " ", "model", "]"}]], "InlineFormula"],
     "  ",
     "Constrain a completed amplitude and its retained correction freedom."}]\
, "Usage"],
  Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"CompleteHighEnergy", "[", "input",
         ",", " ", "channels", ",", " ", "model", ",", " ", "\"Output\"",
         " ", "->", " ", "\"Details\"", "]"}]], "InlineFormula"], "  ", "Insp\
ect the fit equations, affine family and independent validation."}],
   "Usage"], Cell["Details", "NotesSection"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Physical residues do not always determine an amplitude \
completely. A combination of corrections can vanish on all physical poles \
while still changing the amplitude elsewhere. This stage uses high-energy \
growth, spurious-pole cancellation and explicit reference independence to \
constrain the freedom retained by ", ButtonBox["CompleteFactorization",
      BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData ->
       "paclet:HEPCAT/ref/CompleteFactorization"], "."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"For the staged workflow, pass detailed factorization output \
so the remaining family is available. Passing only one displayed amplitude \
can lose alternative directions that were present in the fit. An advanced \
calculation can instead supply a justified ",
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"CorrectionBasis\""}]], "InlineFormula"],
     " explicitly."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Detailed preparation and factorization reports also retain \
the expressions discarded by diagram-local or channel-local shell reductions. \
The high-energy stage can fit those source differences before relying on \
reweighting the prepared pole terms. This matters when the lost piece is \
regular at a physical pole and is therefore invisible to that residue. The \
correction must still satisfy every original physical channel, high-energy \
condition, spurious-pole check and reference test. This does not introduce \
independent contact interactions into the theory."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"When the model has dependent masses or couplings, automatic \
supplementary parameter assignments vary independent inputs and recompute the \
declared functions. Explicit local overrides stay fixed. The same correction \
coefficients are fitted simultaneously across these assignments, so a \
cancellation that occurs only at the default numerical masses does not \
masquerade as a symbolic identity. These are additional exact sampled \
constraints and independent checks, rather than an analytic uniqueness \
proof."}], "Notes"], Cell[TextData[{"Reference-independence fitting uses \
exact rational external on-shell kinematics when available. All massive-spin \
components remain in the fit, momentum conservation is checked exactly, and \
no internal propagator is put on shell. This avoids expensive square-root \
normalization factors in the linear equations. The final candidate still has \
to pass independent physical-kinematics checks; these fitting samples are not \
a proof of reference independence at every point."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"For a supported four-point process containing a massive \
Dirac pair, a scalar and a massless vector, automatic completion first tries \
a minimal cubic-current continuation. The scalar and pseudoscalar couplings \
are recovered from the model's angle- and square-bracket three-point \
bilinears and matched symbolically to their Dirac currents. The vector vertex \
and the original diagram propagators must also match. This bounded first \
attempt retains the inherited factorization freedom and performs every \
independent check. If it does not complete, the remaining stage time is used \
for the broader retained-source fit. A colored current additionally requires \
an exact rank-one fundamental color-flow projection certificate; its \
coordinate and any basis phase are preserved."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"A direction containing auxiliary Xi spinors can be removed \
only after an ordinary identity proof. The symbolic proof keeps independent \
coordinates for every reference label, all coherent external spin components \
and symbolic model parameters. It checks exact rational cancellation with \
external shells and momentum conservation, without sampling reference values \
or imposing internal shells."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"For a large coherent-spin calculation with failed initial \
checks, Automatic can first fit a small set of complete shell differences \
retained by preparation. Each difference keeps its model couplings and \
correlated terms. This attempt shares the stage deadline and the inherited \
factorization family; if it does not complete, the full source space remains \
available. Already-passing inputs skip this proposal, and an explicit \
correction basis stays authoritative. Detailed output records the proposal \
and its result. An exact contradiction in the reached equations rejects that \
proposal immediately; later charts cannot repair it. The report retains the \
contradiction and identifies charts that were not attempted. Accepted \
amplitudes still require all requested charts, parameter assignments and \
independent final checks."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Large retained vector-source columns can be shortened \
before fitting by grouping terms with the same original propagators and \
non-mass couplings. Ordinary spinor identities then see the complete \
external-mass partners together. Each column keeps its single correction \
coefficient; its expanded terms are not assigned independent coefficients. \
Only declared external masses enter this reduction. The original complete \
columns remain available, and a bounded or unsupported reduction leaves its \
original column unchanged."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"For a large affine vector family, repeated typed spinor \
monomials can be expanded once on the same conserved energy chart and \
recombined through their exact original coefficient map. Every source part is \
reconstructed exactly, every Laurent coefficient keeps its remainder-order \
certificate, and all original correction coordinates and constant offsets \
stay coupled. Exact numerical coefficients are simplified by ordinary algebra \
before solving, with an exact replay of every replacement. A failed \
certificate cannot become a passing equation, and the existing expansion \
remains available when the bounded shared calculation is unsupported."}],
   "Notes"], Cell[TextData[{"For larger spin tensors, the high-energy \
equations are formed from separate exact scalar Laurent series. The \
correction coefficients remain coupled in each spin-component equation, \
including its constant term; they are not tested independently or estimated \
from a few spin assignments. For supported large tensors, finite Laurent \
series are used as the coefficient field before the spin polynomial is \
multiplied out. Their remainder orders and conservative leading-power bounds \
are retained through every product, so a positive power that later multiplies \
a negative power is not lost. Every coefficient must cover the requested \
order before its remainder is removed. The same certified engine supports \
fitting and the final high-energy check; a bounded unsupported attempt \
retains the ordinary route, while an unproved scalar-series zero assumption \
remains unresolved."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"After an energy chart fixes some correction coefficients, \
later charts and model-consistent parameter assignments can use the exact \
affine family that remains. The first equations stay in their original \
coordinates, and every later restricted equation is retained alongside them. \
A reduction is used only when its complete free-coordinate map and every \
retained equation are verified exactly; otherwise the previous family remains \
in use. All requested charts, parameter assignments and final independent \
validation checks are still evaluated."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"The ordinary successful output is ",
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", "externalMassRules", ",", " ", "expression",
         "}"}]], "InlineFormula"], ". It can be simplified again with ",
     ButtonBox["PrepareAmplitudes", BaseStyle -> "Link",
      ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/ref/PrepareAmplitudes"],
     ", using ordinary external identities only, and checked with ",
     ButtonBox["CheckAmplitudes", BaseStyle -> "Link",
      ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/ref/CheckAmplitudes"], "."}], "Notes"],
  Cell["The physical conditions", "NotesSection"],
  Cell[TextData[{"The automatic high-energy bound is ",
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"E", "^", "(", "4", "-", "n", ")"}]],
      "InlineFormula"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{" ", "for", " ", "a", " ",
         "renormalizable", " ", "tree", " ", "amplitude", " ", "with",
         " "}]], "InlineFormula"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"n"}]],
      "InlineFormula"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{" ", "external", " ",
         "particles", ".", " ", "The", " ", "comparison", " ", "takes", " ",
         "fixed", "-", "angle", " ", "high", "-", "energy", " ", "limits",
         " ", "while", " ", "holding", " ", "masses", " ", "and", " ",
         "couplings", " ", "fixed", ".", " ", "Supply", " "}]],
      "InlineFormula"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"HighEnergyBound\""}]],
      "InlineFormula"],
     " when your theory requires a different stated growth condition."}],
   "Notes"], Cell[TextData[{"A proposed correction is also checked against \
the original physical channels. Removing a spurious singularity or improving \
energy growth is not allowed to spoil factorization. Explicit Xi references \
are varied when present, and apparent denominator poles must satisfy the \
supported cancellation checks."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Only directions supported by the model and the supplied \
calculation are considered. The function does not create an unrestricted \
contact-interaction basis or infer new QED interactions. If the stated \
constraints leave a genuine free interaction, that freedom must remain \
unresolved rather than being assigned an arbitrary number."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Redundant correction directions are removed only after \
exact identity proofs. The proof portfolio combines ordinary external \
reductions with economical massive-spinor coordinates, checking every \
coherent-spin coefficient. A supported large direction may use the faster \
coordinate proof first. Angle and square basis choices describe the same \
complete spin tensor; they are calculation choices, not assumptions that the \
theory is parity invariant."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"When a conservative high-energy certificate already \
establishes the requested bound for every component, expensive series \
expansions are unnecessary. Fresh validation directions and the supplied \
parameter-validation sets are still checked. A failed or unfinished \
certificate falls back to the existing calculation and never turns an \
unresolved result into PASS."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"A retained factorization direction can be zero by ordinary \
identities even when its displayed expression is large. For a supported \
process with one massless external leg and at least three massive legs, the \
exact proof portfolio can first use a massive rest frame and a null momentum \
axis, then clear coordinate denominators and check the entire coherent spin \
polynomial. This is a generic external-kinematics identity proof with model \
parameters retained, rather than a test at a numerical phase-space point. It \
does not put an internal line on shell. The optional native arithmetic \
backend shares the existing stage deadline; if it is unavailable or cannot \
establish the certificate, the original direction remains available to the \
other proof and completion paths."}], "Notes"],
  Cell["Reading the result", "NotesSection"],
  Cell[TextData[{"Use ", Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"Output\"", " ", "->", " ",
         "\"Details\""}]], "InlineFormula"],
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{" ", "to", " ", "inspect", " ", "the", " ",
         "candidate", ",", " ", "fitted", " ", "family", ",", " ",
         "equations", " ", "and", " ", "independent", " ", "validation", ".",
         " ", "This", " ", "is", " ", "the", " ", "appropriate", " ",
         "output", " ", "when", " ", "an", " ", "ordinary", " ", "call", " ",
         "returns", " "}]], "InlineFormula"],
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Failure"}]], "InlineFormula"], ": it lets you \
distinguish an insufficient direction space, an unfixed coefficient, an \
unsupported check and a computation that reached its time limit."}],
   "Notes"], Cell[TextData[{"Large expressions may be tested through a \
conservative upper bound on their high-energy power rather than an explicitly \
measured leading power. Such a report labels the quantity ",
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ObservedPowerUpperBound\""}]], "InlineFormula"],
     "; it should not be read as an exact leading exponent. The report states \
which method completed."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[{"The code can remove a retained direction after an exact \
identity certificate, but a few numerical zeros do not establish an identity. \
Unfinished proofs keep the direction. The same distinction applies when \
interpreting a successful sampled check of the final amplitude."}], "Notes"],
  Cell[TextData[
    {"Defaults choose finite effort automatically. Set an integer ",
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"RandomSeed\""}]], "InlineFormula"],
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{" ", "to", " ", "replay", " ", "sampled", " ",
         "kinematics", ",", " ", "or", " ", "adjust", " "}]],
      "InlineFormula"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"TimeConstraint\""}]],
      "InlineFormula"], " for a longer calculation. A timeout remains visible \
in the result; it does not silently turn an undetermined family into a \
completed amplitude."}], "Notes"], Cell["Options", "NotesSection"],
  Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{{Cell["Option", "TableHeader"],
       Cell["Default", "TableHeader"], Cell["Meaning", "TableHeader"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"CorrectionBasis\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Explicit correction expressions whose coefficients may \
be fitted. Automatic uses bounded directions from the supplied diagrams, \
channels and retained preparation data; it does not invent an unrestricted \
interaction ansatz."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"PolePreference\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Reference and representation preference shared by \
preparation and completion. ConstructAmplitude defaults to \"AllPhysical\"; \
choose \"AllSpurious\" or \"Mixed\" explicitly for a different preference. \
The individual preparation/completion functions retain their own Automatic \
comparison or inheritance rules. Cyclic/common/independent reference choices \
and None are also available. Required checks remain unchanged."}],
        "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ParameterRules\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Local numerical parameter overrides keyed by model \
references or string names. Automatic resolves the model's variables and \
dependent functions; fitting and checks do not assign global values or \
replace parameters in the symbolic final amplitude."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"Samples\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Number of physical cut samples or fitting samples, \
depending on the stage. Automatic completion settings choose a bounded \
process-dependent count; successful fitting is followed by separate \
validation samples."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ValidationSamples\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Number of held-out samples used after fitting \
correction coefficients. Automatic chooses a bounded workload-dependent \
allowance; these samples are distinct from the fitting equations."}],
        "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"RandomSeed\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Random-sampling seed. Automatic resolves one \
time-based seed for the operation; an explicit integer, including zero, \
reproduces the sample choices. Detailed reports retain the resolved seed. \
ComplicateAmplitude uses the legacy symbol key RandomSeed; modern \
construction/checking functions use the string key \"RandomSeed\"."}],
        "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"TimeConstraint\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Maximum elapsed time allowed by the operation or \
stage. Where Automatic is supported it selects a finite workload-dependent \
budget; explicit seconds or supported Infinity override it. A timeout is \
reported, never treated as a successful amplitude."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"HighEnergyBound\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Maximum allowed power of energy with masses fixed. \
Automatic uses the renormalizable tree target E^(4-n); provide a justified \
different bound for another theory or normalization."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"MaxCorrectionTerms\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Bound on the size of the fitted correction space. \
Automatic chooses a finite process-dependent limit; explicit values control \
effort without weakening residue and independent-validation requirements."}],
        "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"Parallelization\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Whether independent items or checks may use local \
Wolfram workers. Automatic uses suitable parallel work within available \
resources; False requests serial evaluation, and result order is retained."}]\
, "TableText"]}, {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"MaxKernels\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Maximum local Wolfram worker kernels available to \
parallel stages. Automatic respects processor availability and license \
limits; 1 requests serial execution. Worker setup and expression transfer can \
outweigh small jobs."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ValidationParameterRules\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Additional or automatically selected local parameter \
assignments used in independent completion validation. They help detect fits \
tied accidentally to one numerical mass or coupling point without changing \
the symbolic result."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ProgressReporting\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"True"}]], "InlineFormula"]}],
        "TableText"], Cell[TextData[{"Whether preparation and construction \
stages display their progress. False suppresses the live stage display \
without changing the algebra or validation."}], "TableText"]},
      {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"ProgressFunction\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Automatic"}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Optional callback receiving stage progress \
information. Automatic uses the normal progress display; callbacks are \
evaluated on the main kernel rather than independently by workers."}],
        "TableText"]}, {Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"Output\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"\"Amplitude\""}]],
           "InlineFormula"]}], "TableText"],
       Cell[TextData[{"Choose the amount and shape of returned information. \
Defaults are a compact check table, prepared input forms, a completed \
{externalMassRules, expression} pair, or a bare constructed expression; \
\"Details\" preserves diagnostics and provenance."}], "TableText"]}},
     GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {Left, Left, Left}},
     GridBoxItemSize -> {"Columns" -> {Scaled[0.23], Scaled[0.19],
         Scaled[0.58]}}, GridBoxDividers ->
      {"Rows" -> {False, True, {False}, False}}]], "3ColumnTableMod"],
  Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection"],
  Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Load HEPCAT if needed", "ExampleSubsection"],
     Cell["Skip this setup if HEPCAT is already loaded. These examples assume \
the checkout is in ~/code/hepcat; change the paths if yours is elsewhere. \
Evaluate loading in its own cell before the calculation.", "ExampleText"],
     Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Get", "[", "\"~/code/hepcat/source/HEPCAT.wl\"",
         "]", ";", "\n", "modelDirectory", " ", "=", " ",
         "\"~/code/hepcat/models\"", ";"}]], "Input",
      InitializationCell -> True]}, Closed]], Cell["Basic Examples",
   "ExampleSection"], Cell[TextData[{"Retain the factorization family and \
pass it into the high-energy stage. The ordinary output keeps the external \
mass rules next to the completed expression."}], "ExampleText"],
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"qedModel", " ", "=", " ", "ReadModel", "[",
      "modelDirectory", ",", " ", "2", "]", ";", "\n", "process", " ", "=",
      " ", "{", "\"e\"", ",", " ", "\"E\"", ",", " ", "\"A.+\"", ",", " ",
      "\"A.+\"", "}", ";", "\n", "diagrams", " ", "=", " ", "CreateDiagrams",
      "[", "process", ",", " ", "qedModel", "]", ";", "\n", "amplitudes",
      " ", "=", " ", "DiagramAmplitudes", "[", "diagrams", "]", ";", "\n",
      "channels", " ", "=", " ", "FactorizationAmplitudeChannels", "[",
      "process", ",", " ", "qedModel", "]", ";", "\n", "prepared", " ", "=",
      " ", "PrepareAmplitudes", "[", "amplitudes", ",", " ", "qedModel", ",",
      " ", "\"ReturnDetails\"", " ", "->", " ", "True", "]", ";", "\n",
      "factorized", " ", "=", " ", "CompleteFactorization", "[", "prepared",
      ",", " ", "channels", ",", " ", "qedModel", ",", " ",
      "\"ReturnDetails\"", " ", "->", " ", "True", "]", ";", "\n",
      "CompleteHighEnergy", "[", "factorized", ",", " ", "channels", ",",
      " ", "qedModel", "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["This example writes files, \
starts a longer calculation, or requires an optional external tool. Evaluate \
it when you want to perform that operation.", "ExampleText"],
  Cell[TextData[
    {"Retain an allowed four-point scalar contact interaction."}],
   "ExampleText"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"scalarModel", " ", "=", " ", "{",
      "{", "{", "\"scalar\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", ",", " ",
      "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "1", ",",
      " ", "\"\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", "}", "}", ",", " ",
      "{", "{", "\"s\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", ",", " ", "\"s\"", ",", " ",
      "\"s\"", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "7", "}", "}", ",", " ", "{", "}",
      ",", " ", "{", "}", "}", ";", "\n", "externalRules", " ", "=", " ",
      "Thread", "[", "(", "Mass", " ", "/@", " ", "Range", "[", "4", "]",
      ")", " ", "->", " ", "0", "]", ";", "\n", "factorized", " ", "=", " ",
      "CompleteFactorization", "[", "{", "externalRules", ",", " ", "7", "}",
      ",", " ", "{", "}", ",", " ", "scalarModel", ",", " ",
      "\"ExternalParticles\"", " ", "->", " ", "ConstantArray", "[", "\"s\"",
      ",", " ", "4", "]", ",", " ", "\"ReturnDetails\"", " ", "->", " ",
      "True", ",", " ", "\"ProgressReporting\"", " ", "->", " ", "False",
      ",", " ", "\"RandomSeed\"", " ", "->", " ", "2026", "]", ";", "\n",
      "CompleteHighEnergy", "[", "factorized", ",", " ", "{", "}", ",", " ",
      "scalarModel", ",", " ", "\"ProgressReporting\"", " ", "->", " ",
      "False", ",", " ", "\"RandomSeed\"", " ", "->", " ", "2026", "]"}]],
   "Input"], Cell["This example writes files, starts a longer calculation, or \
requires an optional external tool. Evaluate it when you want to perform that \
operation.", "ExampleText"], Cell["See Also", "SeeAlsoSection"],
  Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["CompleteFactorization", BaseStyle -> "Link",
      ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/ref/CompleteFactorization"],
     "  \[Bullet]  ", ButtonBox["CheckAmplitudes", BaseStyle -> "Link",
      ButtonData -> "paclet:HEPCAT/ref/CheckAmplitudes"], "  \[Bullet]  ",
